        #revision
          %p
            “Approximation of the identity” is a rubric for a general technique in functional analysis for proving that certain inclusions of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p We illustrate the technique with two examples.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Consider first the problem of showing that restrictions of polynomials to
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-1, 1]
              are dense in
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p([-1, 1])
              (under Lebesgue
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                      %mn 1
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(y) = (f * \delta)(y) = \int_{-1}^1 f(x) \delta(y - x) d x
            %p
              (which literally makes no sense because
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
              is not an actual integrable function) and then replace
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
              by polynomial functions
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_n
              which “approximate” to it (so each
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_n
              has “mass” 1 and is vanishingly small outside a given neighborhood of 0, if
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is sufficiently large).
            %p
              Put for example
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n(x) = (1 - x^2)^n
              and “normalize” it, putting
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_n = \frac{f_n}{\|f_n\|_1}
            %p
              where
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|g\|_1
              indicates
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
              norm. By “differentiating under the integral sign”, we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi j
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^j (f * p_n) = (-1)^j f * D^j(p_n)
            %p
              so that for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi j
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j^{th}
              derivative of
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f * p_n
              is identically zero for
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              sufficiently large. Hence
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f * p_n
              is polynomial. Next, the claim is that for
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in L^p
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim_{n \to \infty} \|f - (f * p_n)\|_p = 0
            %p
              Intuitively, the idea is that
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f - (f * p_n) = f * (\delta - p_n)
              and that (because
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p
              is a module over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi p
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi δ
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|f * (\delta - p_n)\|_p \leq \|f\|_p \|\delta - p_n\|_1 \to 0
            %p
              as
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \to \infty
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For a second example, consider how to prove that the functions
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^n
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              ranging over integers, forms an orthonormal basis of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(S^1)
              where
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
              is the unit circle in the complex plane, where the inner product is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi z
                      %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, g \rangle = \frac1{2\pi i} \int_{S^1} \overline{f(z)} g(z) \frac{d z}{z}
            %p
              The monomials
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^n
              are clearly orthonormal, so again the idea is to use appropriate linear combinations of the
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^n
              (i.e., Laurent polynomials) to approximate a Dirac mass concentrated at the identity
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = 1
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi k
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi …
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mi …
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n(z) = \frac1{n+1} (\sum_{-n \leq k \leq n} z^{k/2})^2 = \frac1{n+1}(z^{-n} + 2z^{-n+1} + \ldots + (n+1)z^0 + \ldots + 2z^{n-1} + z^n)
            %p
              Each Laurent polynomial
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n(z)
              is real-valued, nonnegative, and its
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
              norm is 1. Putting
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = e^{i x}
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi sin
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi sin
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n(e^{i x}) = \frac1{n+1} \frac{\sin^2((n+1)x/2)}{\sin^2(x/2)}
            %p
              which makes it clear that
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n(e^{i x})
              becomes very small outside a neighborhood of 0 (in
              = succeed ")" do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              grows large. Thus
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
              approximates the identity; therefore for any
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
              function
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_47.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo *
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{n \to \infty} \|(F_n * f) - f\|_2 = 0
            %p
              Finally,
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_48.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi n
                    %mo *
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n * f
              is itself a Laurent polynomial; this follows from the fact that for the function
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_n(z) = z^n
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mi n
                    %mo *
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi z
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mi z
                      %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e_n * f)(w) = \frac1{2\pi i}\int_{S^1} (w/z)^n f(z) \frac{d z}{z} = e_n(w)\langle e_n, f \rangle
            %p
              It follows from all this that the Laurent polynomials on
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
              are dense in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi L
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(S^1)
            %p
              A similar technique applies to any compact Hausdorff abelian group
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_53.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              equipped with its normalized Haar measure
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_54.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_55.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi z
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S^1, \frac1{2\pi i}\frac{d z}{z})
              %math#mathml_82a228d6bac48d1e07ac627d34d30d6db34ac01a_56.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
              norm. In other words, the characters form an orthonormal basis of
              = succeed "." do
